{"book":"ISA","chapter":20,"source":"hbo","verses":[1,2,3,4,5,6],"records":[{"id":"ISA.20.1.s1","verses":[1],"source":"בִּשְׁנַ֨ת בֹּ֤א תַרְתָּן֙ אַשְׁדּ֔וֹדָה בִּשְׁלֹ֣ח אֹת֔וֹ סַֽרְג֖וֹן מֶ֣לֶךְ אַשּׁ֑וּר וַיִּלָּ֥חֶם בְּאַשְׁדּ֖וֹד וַֽיִּלְכְּדָֽהּ׃","gloss":"In [the] year came [the] commander-in-chief to Ashdod, when sent him Sargon king of Assyria, and he fought against Ashdod and captured it,","pieces":[[1,"In the year that the commander-in-chief came to Ashdod, sent by Sargon king of Assyria, and fought against Ashdod and captured it,",null]],"L":{"1":"In the year that [the] commander-in-chief came to Ashdod, when Sargon king of Assyria sent him, and he fought against Ashdod and captured it,"},"R":{"1":"Sargon, the king of Assyria, sent the commander of his army to attack the city of Ashdod. The commander fought against Ashdod and captured it."},"decisions":[{"id":"m1","layer":"meaning","levels":null,"category":"culture","tokens":["o230200010071","o230200010081","o230200010091"],"choice":"Sargon king of Assyria: Sargon II, who reigned from 722 to 705 BC","alternatives":[],"reason":"This is the only place in Scripture where Sargon is named. His own inscriptions tell how Ashdod rebelled against Assyria, trusting in help from Egypt, and how his army took the city, about 711 BC.","uncertain":false,"footnote":false},{"id":"m2","layer":"meaning","levels":null,"category":"word_sense","tokens":["o230200010031"],"choice":"commander-in-chief: an Assyrian title, the king's highest officer","alternatives":["the Tartan"],"reason":"The word is an Assyrian title for the top army commander, the second man in the kingdom. It is used again in 2 Kings 18:17, where Sennacherib's commander comes to Jerusalem.","uncertain":false,"footnote":false},{"id":"m3","layer":"meaning","levels":null,"category":"culture","tokens":["o230200010041","o230200010042"],"choice":"Ashdod: one of the five Philistine cities","alternatives":[],"reason":"Ashdod lay on the coast west of Judah (1 Samuel 6:17). Its revolt and fall are the setting for the sign in verses 2-6.","uncertain":false,"footnote":false},{"id":"r-en-1","layer":"rendering","levels":["R"],"category":"word_order","tokens":["o230200010011","o230200010012"],"choice":"Sargon ... sent ... captured it.","alternatives":[],"reason":"Readable tells the events as two sentences; verse 2 then says \"At that time\".","uncertain":false,"footnote":false}],"heading":"Naked and barefoot as a sign","status":"ai_draft"},{"id":"ISA.20.2.s1","verses":[2],"source":"בָּעֵ֣ת הַהִ֗יא דִּבֶּ֣ר יְהוָה֮ בְּיַ֣ד יְשַׁעְיָ֣הוּ בֶן־אָמוֹץ֮ לֵאמֹר֒ לֵ֗ךְ וּפִתַּחְתָּ֤ הַשַּׂק֙ מֵעַ֣ל מָתְנֶ֔יךָ וְנַעַלְךָ֥ תַחֲלֹ֖ץ מֵעַ֣ל רַגְלֶ֑יךָ וַיַּ֣עַשׂ כֵּ֔ן הָלֹ֖ךְ עָר֥וֹם וְיָחֵֽף׃ס","gloss":"at the time the that spoke Yahweh by [the] hand of Isaiah son of Amoz, saying, go and loosen the sackcloth from on your loins, and your sandal take off from on your feet. And he did so, walking naked and barefoot.","pieces":[[2,"at that time {{divine_name:the LORD|Yahweh}} spoke through Isaiah son of Amoz: \"Go, untie the sackcloth from around your waist, and pull the sandals off your feet.\" He did as he was told, walking about naked and barefoot.",null]],"L":{"2":"at that time {{divine_name:the LORD|Yahweh}} spoke by the hand of Isaiah son of Amoz, saying, \"Go, and loosen the sackcloth from your waist, and take your sandals off your feet.\" And he did so, walking naked and barefoot."},"R":{"2":"At that time {{divine_name:the LORD|Yahweh}} gave a message through Isaiah son of Amoz. {{divine_name:The LORD|Yahweh}} told him, \"Go and take off the rough cloth you wear around your waist, and take off your sandals.\" Isaiah obeyed. He walked around stripped, with bare feet."},"decisions":[{"id":"m1","layer":"meaning","levels":null,"category":"idiom","tokens":["o230200020051","o230200020052","o230200020061"],"choice":"by the hand of Isaiah: through Isaiah","alternatives":[],"reason":"\"By the hand of\" means through someone as messenger. Here God's word first comes to Isaiah himself as a command, and his action becomes the message.","uncertain":false,"footnote":false},{"id":"m2","layer":"meaning","levels":null,"category":"culture","tokens":["o230200020122"],"choice":"sackcloth: rough cloth worn as a sign of mourning or as a prophet's garment","alternatives":[],"reason":"Sackcloth was worn in grief (22:12; 37:1). Prophets were known for rough clothing (2 Kings 1:8; Zechariah 13:4). Isaiah's wearing it here may be a sign of mourning or simply his usual dress.","uncertain":false,"footnote":false},{"id":"m3","layer":"meaning","levels":null,"category":"word_sense","tokens":["o230200020221"],"choice":"naked: stripped of his outer garment","alternatives":["fully naked"],"reason":"The word can mean fully naked, or wearing only an undergarment (as in 1 Samuel 19:24 and John 21:7). Captives were led away stripped (verse 4).","uncertain":true,"footnote":true},{"id":"r-en-1","layer":"rendering","levels":["R"],"category":"word_choice","tokens":["o230200020122"],"choice":"rough cloth","alternatives":[],"reason":"Readable explains sackcloth as rough cloth.","uncertain":false,"footnote":false}],"heading":null,"status":"ai_draft"},{"id":"ISA.20.3.s1","verses":[3],"source":"וַיֹּ֣אמֶר יְהוָ֔ה כַּאֲשֶׁ֥ר הָלַ֛ךְ עַבְדִּ֥י יְשַׁעְיָ֖הוּ עָר֣וֹם וְיָחֵ֑ף שָׁלֹ֤שׁ שָׁנִים֙ א֣וֹת וּמוֹפֵ֔ת עַל־מִצְרַ֖יִם וְעַל־כּֽוּשׁ׃","gloss":"And said Yahweh, just as has walked my servant Isaiah naked and barefoot three years, a sign and a portent against Egypt and against Cush,","pieces":[[3,"Then {{divine_name:the LORD|Yahweh}} said, \"My servant Isaiah has walked about naked and barefoot for three years, a sign and a forewarning against Egypt and Cush.",null]],"L":{"3":"And {{divine_name:the LORD|Yahweh}} said, \"Just as {{deity_pronoun:my|My}} servant Isaiah has walked naked and barefoot three years, a sign and a portent against Egypt and against Cush,"},"R":{"3":"Then {{divine_name:the LORD|Yahweh}} said, \"My servant Isaiah has walked around stripped and barefoot for three years. This is a sign and a warning of what will happen to Egypt and Cush."},"decisions":[{"id":"m1","layer":"meaning","levels":null,"category":"grammar","tokens":["o230200030091","o230200030101"],"choice":"three years: how long Isaiah went stripped","alternatives":["a sign for three years"],"reason":"The phrase may go with \"walked\", so that Isaiah went stripped and barefoot for three years, or with \"a sign\", a sign of three years, perhaps showing on and off how long the trouble would last.","uncertain":true,"footnote":true},{"id":"m2","layer":"meaning","levels":null,"category":"word_sense","tokens":["o230200030111","o230200030122"],"choice":"a sign and a portent: an act that points ahead to what God will do","alternatives":[],"reason":"The same pair is used of Isaiah and his children in 8:18. A portent is a startling sign of things to come.","uncertain":false,"footnote":false},{"id":"m3","layer":"meaning","levels":null,"category":"culture","tokens":["o230200030161"],"choice":"Cush: the land south of Egypt, on the upper Nile","alternatives":[],"reason":"In Isaiah's time kings from Cush ruled Egypt as well, so the two are named together (18:1-2; 37:9).","uncertain":false,"footnote":false},{"id":"r-en-1","layer":"rendering","levels":["B"],"category":"word_order","tokens":["o230200030031","o230200030032"],"choice":"a full stop, then \"In the same way\"","alternatives":[],"reason":"Balanced ends the sentence here and begins verse 4 with \"In the same way\" for the Hebrew \"just as ... so\".","uncertain":false,"footnote":false}],"heading":null,"status":"ai_draft"},{"id":"ISA.20.4.s1","verses":[4],"source":"כֵּ֣ן יִנְהַ֣ג מֶֽלֶךְ־אַ֠שּׁוּר אֶת־שְׁבִ֨י מִצְרַ֜יִם וְאֶת־גָּל֥וּת כּ֛וּשׁ נְעָרִ֥ים וּזְקֵנִ֖ים עָר֣וֹם וְיָחֵ֑ף וַחֲשׂוּפַ֥י שֵׁ֖ת עֶרְוַ֥ת מִצְרָֽיִם׃","gloss":"so will lead away [the] king of Assyria [the] captives of Egypt and [the] exiles of Cush, young men and old men, naked and barefoot, and bared of buttock, [the] nakedness of Egypt.","pieces":[[4,"In the same way the king of Assyria will drive off Egypt's captives and Cush's exiles, young and old, naked and barefoot, with their buttocks uncovered, the disgrace of Egypt.",null]],"L":{"4":"so the king of Assyria will lead away the captives of Egypt and the exiles of Cush, young and old, naked and barefoot, with buttocks bared, the nakedness of Egypt."},"R":{"4":"In the same way, the king of Assyria will lead away the people of Egypt and Cush as prisoners. Young and old will walk naked and barefoot, with their backsides bare. Egypt will be disgraced."},"decisions":[{"id":"m1","layer":"meaning","levels":null,"category":"culture","tokens":["o230200040021","o230200040061","o230200040091"],"choice":"lead away captives and exiles: prisoners of war driven into exile","alternatives":[],"reason":"Assyrian kings deported conquered peoples, and their carvings show captives led away stripped. The same fate came to the northern kingdom of Israel in 722 BC (2 Kings 17:6).","uncertain":false,"footnote":false},{"id":"m2","layer":"meaning","levels":null,"category":"idiom","tokens":["o230200040171","o230200040181"],"choice":"the nakedness of Egypt: Egypt's shame","alternatives":[],"reason":"\"Nakedness\" stands for disgrace and exposure (47:3; Nahum 3:5). The proud nation is put to open shame.","uncertain":false,"footnote":false},{"id":"r-en-1","layer":"rendering","levels":["B"],"category":"word_choice","tokens":["o230200040171"],"choice":"the disgrace of Egypt","alternatives":[],"reason":"Balanced gives the sense of \"nakedness\" here; Literal keeps the word.","uncertain":false,"footnote":false}],"heading":null,"status":"ai_draft"},{"id":"ISA.20.5.s1","verses":[5],"source":"וְחַתּ֖וּ וָבֹ֑שׁוּ מִכּוּשׁ֙ מַבָּטָ֔ם וּמִן־מִצְרַ֖יִם תִּפְאַרְתָּֽם׃","gloss":"And they will be dismayed and ashamed because of Cush their hope, and because of Egypt their boast.","pieces":[[5,"Then they will be shattered and disgraced, because Cush was their hope and Egypt their pride.",null]],"L":{"5":"And they will be dismayed and ashamed because of Cush their hope and because of Egypt their boast."},"R":{"5":"People who were counting on Cush and were proud of Egypt will be afraid and ashamed."},"decisions":[{"id":"m1","layer":"meaning","levels":null,"category":"grammar","tokens":["o230200050012","o230200050022"],"choice":"they: those who relied on Egypt and Cush","alternatives":[],"reason":"The subject is not named. Verse 6 shows that it includes the people of the coast, Ashdod and its neighbors, who had looked to Egypt for help against Assyria. Judah too was tempted to trust Egypt (30:1-3; 31:1).","uncertain":false,"footnote":false},{"id":"m2","layer":"meaning","levels":null,"category":"word_sense","tokens":["o230200050041","o230200050042","o230200050071","o230200050072"],"choice":"their hope ... their boast: what they trusted in and were proud of","alternatives":[],"reason":"The first word means something one looks to with confidence. The second means beauty or glory, that in which one takes pride.","uncertain":false,"footnote":false},{"id":"r-en-1","layer":"rendering","levels":["R"],"category":"supplied_words","tokens":["o230200050012"],"choice":"People who were counting on","alternatives":[],"reason":"Readable names the subject from the phrases \"their hope\" and \"their boast\".","uncertain":false,"footnote":false}],"heading":null,"status":"ai_draft"},{"id":"ISA.20.6.s1","verses":[6],"source":"וְ֠אָמַר יֹשֵׁ֨ב הָאִ֣י הַזֶּה֮ בַּיּ֣וֹם הַהוּא֒ הִנֵּה־כֹ֣ה מַבָּטֵ֗נוּ אֲשֶׁר־נַ֤סְנוּ שָׁם֙ לְעֶזְרָ֔ה לְהִ֨נָּצֵ֔ל מִפְּנֵ֖י מֶ֣לֶךְ אַשּׁ֑וּר וְאֵ֖יךְ נִמָּלֵ֥ט אֲנָֽחְנוּ׃ס","gloss":"And will say [the] inhabitant of the coast the this in day the that, look, thus [is our] hope, where we fled there for help to be delivered from before [the] king of Assyria; and how will we escape, we?","pieces":[[6,"On that day those who live along this coast will say, 'Look at the fate of the ones we put our hope in, the ones we ran to for help, to save us from the king of Assyria! So how are we to escape?'\"",null]],"L":{"6":"And the inhabitant of this coastland will say in that day, 'Look, such is our hope, where we fled for help to be delivered from the king of Assyria! And how will we escape, we?'\""},"R":{"6":"At that time the people who live along this coast will say, 'Look what has happened to the nations we trusted! We ran to them for help to save us from the king of Assyria. Now how can we escape?'\""},"decisions":[{"id":"m1","layer":"meaning","levels":null,"category":"word_sense","tokens":["o230200060031","o230200060032","o230200060041","o230200060042"],"choice":"this coastland: the coast of the Philistines","alternatives":[],"reason":"The word means a coast or a land by the sea. Here \"this\" coast is the land of Ashdod and the other Philistine cities (verse 1), and perhaps its neighbors.","uncertain":false,"footnote":false},{"id":"m2","layer":"meaning","levels":null,"category":"idiom","tokens":["o230200060071","o230200060081","o230200060091","o230200060092"],"choice":"look, such is our hope: see what has become of the ones we trusted","alternatives":[],"reason":"The words point to Egypt and Cush led away as captives. If they could not save themselves, they cannot save anyone else.","uncertain":false,"footnote":false},{"id":"r-en-1","layer":"rendering","levels":["R"],"category":"supplied_words","tokens":["o230200060091","o230200060092"],"choice":"the nations we trusted","alternatives":[],"reason":"Readable says \"nations\" for \"our hope\", meaning Egypt and Cush (verse 5).","uncertain":false,"footnote":false}],"heading":null,"status":"ai_draft"}],"words":{"1":[{"id":"o230200010011","text":"בִּ","after":"","lemma":"בְּ","morph":"R","gloss":"in","strong":"0871a","transliteration":"bi","lang":"H","parse":"Preposition"},{"id":"o230200010012","text":"שְׁנַ֨ת","after":" ","lemma":"שָׁנָה","morph":"Ncfsc","gloss":"the.year","strong":"8141","transliteration":"šənaṯ","lang":"H","parse":"Noun · common · feminine singular construct (of ...)"},{"id":"o230200010021","text":"בֹּ֤א","after":" ","lemma":"בּוֹא","morph":"Vqc","gloss":"came","strong":"0935","transliteration":"bōʾ","lang":"H","parse":"Verb · Qal · infinitive construct"},{"id":"o230200010031","text":"תַרְתָּן֙","after":" ","lemma":"תַּרְתָּן","morph":"Np","gloss":"[the].commander.in.chief","strong":"8661","transliteration":"ṯartān","lang":"H","parse":"Noun · proper name"},{"id":"o230200010041","text":"אַשְׁדּ֔וֹדָ","after":"","lemma":"אַשְׁדּוֹד","morph":"Np","gloss":"to","strong":"0795","transliteration":"","lang":"H","parse":"Noun · proper name"},{"id":"o230200010042","text":"ה","after":" ","lemma":"ה","morph":"Sd","gloss":"Ashdod","strong":"1886c","transliteration":"","lang":"H","parse":"Suffix · directional ending (toward)"},{"id":"o230200010051","text":"בִּ","after":"","lemma":"בְּ","morph":"R","gloss":"when","strong":"0871a","transliteration":"bi","lang":"H","parse":"Preposition"},{"id":"o230200010052","text":"שְׁלֹ֣ח","after":" ","lemma":"שָׁלַח","morph":"Vqc","gloss":"sent","strong":"7971","transliteration":"šəlōḥ","lang":"H","parse":"Verb · Qal · infinitive construct"},{"id":"o230200010061","text":"אֹת֔","after":"","lemma":"אֵת","morph":"To","gloss":"","strong":"0853","transliteration":"","lang":"H","parse":"Particle · direct object marker"},{"id":"o230200010062","text":"וֹ","after":" ","lemma":"הוּא","morph":"Sp3ms","gloss":"him","strong":"2050c","transliteration":"","lang":"H","parse":"Suffix · pronoun · 3rd person masculine singular"},{"id":"o230200010071","text":"סַֽרְג֖וֹן","after":" ","lemma":"סַרְגוֹן","morph":"Np","gloss":"Sargon","strong":"5623","transliteration":"sarəḡôn","lang":"H","parse":"Noun · proper name"},{"id":"o230200010081","text":"מֶ֣לֶךְ","after":" ","lemma":"מֶלֶךְ","morph":"Ncmsc","gloss":"the.king","strong":"4428","transliteration":"meleḵ","lang":"H","parse":"Noun · common · masculine singular construct (of ...)"},{"id":"o230200010091","text":"אַשּׁ֑וּר","after":" ","lemma":"אַשּׁוּר","morph":"Np","gloss":"of.Assyria","strong":"0804","transliteration":"ʾaššûr","lang":"H","parse":"Noun · proper name"},{"id":"o230200010101","text":"וַ","after":"","lemma":"וְ","morph":"C","gloss":"and","strong":"2050b","transliteration":"wa","lang":"H","parse":"Conjunction"},{"id":"o230200010102","text":"יִּלָּ֥חֶם","after":" ","lemma":"לָחַם","morph":"VNw3ms","gloss":"he.fought","strong":"3898","transliteration":"yyillāḥem","lang":"H","parse":"Verb · Niphal · past narrative (wayyiqtol) · 3rd person masculine singular"},{"id":"o230200010111","text":"בְּ","after":"","lemma":"בְּ","morph":"R","gloss":"against","strong":"0871a","transliteration":"bə","lang":"H","parse":"Preposition"},{"id":"o230200010112","text":"אַשְׁדּ֖וֹד","after":" ","lemma":"אַשְׁדּוֹד","morph":"Np","gloss":"Ashdod","strong":"0795","transliteration":"ʾašdôḏ","lang":"H","parse":"Noun · proper name"},{"id":"o230200010121","text":"וַֽ","after":"","lemma":"וְ","morph":"C","gloss":"and","strong":"2050b","transliteration":"wa","lang":"H","parse":"Conjunction"},{"id":"o230200010122","text":"יִּלְכְּדָֽ","after":"","lemma":"לָכַד","morph":"Vqw3ms","gloss":"he","strong":"3920","transliteration":"","lang":"H","parse":"Verb · Qal · past narrative (wayyiqtol) · 3rd person masculine singular"},{"id":"o230200010123","text":"הּ","after":"׃","lemma":"הוּא","morph":"Sp3fs","gloss":"captured.it","strong":"1886d","transliteration":"","lang":"H","parse":"Suffix · pronoun · 3rd person feminine singular"}],"2":[{"id":"o230200020011","text":"בָּ","after":"","lemma":"בְּ","morph":"Rd","gloss":"at","strong":"0871a","transliteration":"bā","lang":"H","parse":"Preposition · with the article"},{"id":"o230200020012","text":"עֵ֣ת","after":" ","lemma":"עֵת","morph":"Ncbsa","gloss":"time","strong":"6256","transliteration":"ʿēṯ","lang":"H","parse":"Noun · common · masculine or feminine singular absolute"},{"id":"o230200020021","text":"הַ","after":"","lemma":"הַ","morph":"Td","gloss":"(the)","strong":"1886a","transliteration